+**The orchestrator-owned record is the routing source.** The host publishes it on
+`MarkRunning`, then revokes it on `MarkStopping`. Client-proxy has no fallback to
+the unread API-owned record; a miss goes to the auto-resume path. Build sandboxes
+are never published. The legacy API/cluster-edge writers remain unchanged until
+removed together; they are not part of the renewable connectivity guarantee.
+
+Ordinary allocations retain their full maximum-lifetime route TTL. Allocations
+admitted with `renewable_lifetime` instead follow the current finite `endAt`.
+A single host reconciliation loop republishes those leases every five seconds,
+and retries failed initial publication and revocation. Errors remain visible in
+logs and routing counters. A route is not proof that a guest is running; the
+live registry still owns execution.
+
+Each publication has an increasing revision and an absolute deadline. Redis
+atomically rejects older revisions, a stopped lifecycle, expired grants, and a
+route owned by another execution/lifecycle. Checkpoint replacement keeps the API
+execution ID but receives a new lifecycle ID; revocation must distinguish them. The per-lifecycle fence lives until the
+largest deadline ever issued, including requests whose outcome is unknown. It
+must outlive a shortened route: otherwise an older delayed renewal could bring
+that route back. Revocation is retained and retried until confirmed or every
+previously issued grant has expired. Route and fence keys share a Redis Cluster
+slot. Redis state loss is a runtime-service failure, outside relay-outage survival.
+
+Renewable lifetime is an operator-only API admission policy via
+`LAUNCHPAD_RENEWABLE_LIFETIME_TEAMS`. The API persists the decision in Redis,
+paused configuration and the orchestrator create contract. A metadata label
+cannot grant it; missing fields remain bounded. Same-allocation resume preserves
+an existing grant after allowlist removal; a fork needs a fresh admission. Removing
+a team blocks new grants, not active busy work. Drain allocations explicitly
+before retiring that team's policy. Restored disk activation captures the policy
+in its immutable create request. Every renewal stays finite and bounded by the
+captured team limit. The host keeper must continue renewing: `endAt`, explicit
+pause/delete, and expired-lease rejection still apply.
+
+Deploy the matching API and orchestrator before admitting renewable allocations.
+The policy alone is not the product readiness gate; a rebuild must pass the
+lifetime-boundary and fresh-connection proof with the keeper enabled.
 
